# Structured Content Extraction Plane — Architectural Invariants

These rules are non-negotiable. Violating any of them breaks the contract with the Synanton
platform. Section references are to the v1.21 proposal (`§n`) and its architectural invariants
(`§67.n`).

## 1. Contract over topology (§67.1)

The `synanton.extraction.v1` contract MUST be identical whether extraction runs embedded in the
platform, co-located, or as an independently scaled cluster. Deployment topology is a scaling
concern, never a contract concern.

The protos in `java/extraction-contract/src/main/proto/` are a **byte-identical mirror** of the
copy in `../platform`. Never edit one side alone. `./scripts/verify-contract-mirror.sh` enforces
this and is wired into `check`.

> The `synanton.gpu.v1` pair drifted exactly this way — one file vs four, `org.*` vs `com.*`,
> different RPC names — because nothing checked. Do not repeat it here.

## 2. Black-box extraction (§67.2)

The platform MUST NOT be able to tell which parser, library, accelerator, queue, or worker
performed the work. Forbidden in any contract identifier, message, field, enum value, or error
code: `opendataloader`, `tika`, `tesseract`, `pdfbox`, `whisper`, `ffmpeg`, `vllm`, `worker`,
`pool`, `broker`, `kafka`, `redis`, `kubernetes`, `pod`, `replica`, `shard`, `gpu`, `cuda`,
`device_id`, `equalix`.

`ContractOpacityTest` walks the compiled descriptors and fails the build on any of these.

## 3. Raw source authority (§67.3)

The plane MUST NOT modify, move, or delete the source object. Source bytes remain the single
authoritative artifact. Adapters receive a read-only stream.

## 4. Domain isolation

`domain/` MUST NOT import protobuf classes, JDBC/Spring, the S3 SDK, Tika, OpenDataLoader, or any
`adapter/` package. The domain crosses only its own ports: `OperationRepository`,
`IdempotencyStore`, `SourceObjectReader`, `PayloadWriter`, `ModalityAdapter`.

Repositories return domain objects, never protobuf. Protobuf conversion happens in
`adapter/in/grpc/` only.

## 5. PostgreSQL is the authoritative operation store

Operation and item state live exclusively in PostgreSQL. No in-memory cache, Redis, Kafka, or
Cassandra may hold or replicate operation state. Redis MUST NOT be introduced for state,
idempotency, admission counters, or any other plane concern — PostgreSQL advisory locks handle
admission serialization.

## 6. Idempotency is required (§67.6)

Every asynchronous submit carries an idempotency key, and the store is **fail-closed**: if the
idempotency store is unavailable, reject the submit. Never admit expensive work whose duplicate
status is unknown.

- same key + same request → the same operation
- same key + materially different request → `ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST`, original untouched

## 7. Feature state must be honest (§67.10)

A requested feature that was not applied MUST NOT be reported as success by omission. Compute
feature state from what the processor **actually produced**, never from what was requested.

`ocr = true` on the request tells a caller nothing on its own. The result must say `FEATURE_APPLIED`,
`FEATURE_NOT_APPLICABLE`, `FEATURE_UNSUPPORTED`, `FEATURE_FAILED`, or `FEATURE_PARTIAL`.

## 8. Errors are contract codes, not implementation detail (§24)

Consumers branch only on `ExtractionErrorCode`. A parser NPE is a `diagnostic` string attached to
`ERROR_EXTRACTION_FAILED`; it is never its own code. Never let an implementation-specific string
become load-bearing for callers.

## 9. Expiry is a lifecycle outcome, not a failure (§12)

An operation past `expires_at` reports `STATUS_EXPIRED`, never `STATUS_FAILED`. Before execution and
while queued, expiry cancels the work without starting it. While running, the plane MAY let the
work finish when cancelling costs more than completing.

## 10. No consumer reparse (§67.17)

`flattened_text` MUST be derived from the structured payload. A consumer that wants only text MUST
NOT cause the source to be extracted a second time.

## 11. Extraction is not knowledge processing (§67.18)

Out of scope in this repository: ontology management, entity resolution, canonical business
entities, relationship inference, business classification, ranking, workflow decisions, permanent
knowledge storage.

OCR'ing "Acme Corporation" is extraction. Resolving it to `Company#1234` is not — that belongs to
the platform.

## 12. Business metadata is opaque (§67.11)

`metadata`, `business_tags`, and unknown `routing_tags` are carried, stored, and returned unchanged.
The plane MUST NOT attach domain semantics to keys like `ticketId` or `caseId`.

## 13. Untrusted content

Source content is hostile until proven otherwise. Enforce max object size **before** download, max
processing duration, and max output payload size. Never execute code supplied by source content.
A malicious artifact must yield a contract error code — never a hang, an OOM, or a crashed gateway.

## 14. Sync must not become a second implementation (§19)

`ExtractSync` is submit-and-await over the same domain path as the async flow. It MUST NOT have its
own extraction path or its own result model.

## 15. No scheduler dependency (§67.9)

Only scheduling *intent* (`PriorityClass`) crosses the contract. Equalix or any other scheduler
remains optional, wired via configuration, behind a domain interface. Domain code MUST NOT
reference a scheduler implementation directly.

## 16. No webhooks in v1.21 (§67.15)

Notification is operation id + `GetOperations` + `ListCompletedOperations` cursor polling. Do not
add callbacks, and do not make a future notification mechanism a contract dependency.

## 17. Sensitive data MUST NOT be logged

Never log document text, transcripts, OCR output, generated descriptions, or payload bytes. Log
only: `operation_id`, `content_ref_id`, `tenant_id`, `media_type`, `processor_id`, state
transitions, error codes, durations, byte counts.

## 18. State transitions are validated by the domain

`OperationState.canTransitionTo(next)` guards every state change. Adapters call the guard before
persisting. An invalid state must be unreachable through normal code paths.

## 19. Admission decisions are transactionally safe

The admission check and the operation INSERT execute in a single PostgreSQL transaction under a
tenant-level advisory lock:

```sql
SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(?)   -- tenant lock key
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM extraction_operations WHERE tenant_id = ? AND status NOT IN (terminal...)
INSERT INTO extraction_operations ...
```

`COUNT` alone races: two submits can both read the same free slot and both insert. The advisory
lock closes it.

## 20. No dependency on the GPU plane

This repository MUST NOT depend on `synanton.gpu.v1` or reach the GPU Execution Plane through the
platform. If extraction wants accelerated processing, that is its own internal decision behind its
own boundary.
